<svelte:head><title>Data and privacy · ISCN Authenticator</title><meta name="description" content="How browser validation, account information, sessions, and optional billing data are handled by the ISCN Authenticator website." /></svelte:head>
<div class="page-shell text-page">
	<header class="page-heading"><p class="section-label">Data and privacy</p><h1>Know where<br />your information goes.</h1><p>This page describes the hosted website and customer API. Self-hosted services have their own deployment and data-handling configuration.</p></header>
	<section><h2>Information entered in the workspace</h2><p>Single, batch and report checks run in your browser, as do the FISH builder and CNV evidence worksheet. These tools do not send notation, narratives, evidence entries or reviewer names to a validation server, or save them to account history or browser storage. Inputs stay in page memory while you work.</p><p>CSV and JSON exports are downloaded only when you request them. You control the resulting files and where they are shared. Example links can contain notation in the URL; URLs are included in requests to the website.</p></section>
	<section><h2>Account information</h2><p>Requesting a code sends your email address to the website and Cloudflare Email Service. The application stores a short-lived challenge containing your email, purpose, a keyed code hash, attempt count and expiry. It does not log the code or email body. The challenge also needs a Secure, HttpOnly browser cookie and expires after ten minutes. Expired challenges are deleted in batches on later code requests. Email-address and network-address hashes support sending limits for up to a day; expired limit records are also removed on later requests. After verification, your account stores an identifier, email, verification time, creation date and plan information.</p><p>Signing in creates a session record and a Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax cookie. The session is valid for up to 30 days. Signing out deletes that session and clears its cookie.</p></section>
	<section><h2>Hosting and billing</h2><p>Cloudflare hosts the website and its account database. Requests necessarily reach the hosting infrastructure, including their URL and network metadata. This application does not specify a fixed hosting-log retention period.</p><p>When paid checkout is available, Stripe handles payment entry. Account email and billing identifiers are used to create and manage checkout or portal sessions. Karyotype inputs are not included in these billing requests.</p></section>
	<section><h2>Using the tools responsibly</h2><p>Use non-identifying notation and review content. Do not include patient names, accession numbers or other patient-identifying information. Reviewer names entered for local exports remain part of the exported record. The browser workspace is not a patient-record system.</p><p>Use <a href="/recover">email recovery</a> to regain access and sign out other sessions. If you cannot access the inbox, contact iscn@bioinformat.org for identity review. Self-service account deletion is not provided. Read the <a href="/docs/accounts-and-keys">account guide</a> for migration and recovery details.</p></section>
	<section><h2>Hosted API requests</h2><p>API requests send karyotype notation to our Cloudflare service for processing. The application does not store request bodies or validation results. It stores hashed integration keys, key labels and timestamps, subscription entitlement, aggregate validation counts per subscription period, and pending Checkout parameters (account and billing identifiers, email, Price and callback URLs) used to prevent duplicate purchases. Operational logs contain request IDs, operation, HTTP method, status and timing; the application does not log notation or authorization headers.</p><p>Use notation without patient identifiers. Your integration controls any results it saves. Hosting-provider security and access metadata are separate from application logs.</p></section>
	<section><h2>Further information</h2><p>The <a href="/docs/data-handling">data-handling guide</a> explains the differences between the browser tools, account requests and a self-hosted API. Source code and product issue reporting are available in the <a href="https://github.com/nuin/iscn-authenticator">project repository</a>. Do not include account keys, private addresses or patient information in public issues.</p></section>
</div>
